<section class="nc-guide hub-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>2 measures, 2 different questions</h2>
    <p><a href="../../calculators/bmi.html">BMI</a> asks whether your weight is in a typical range for your height. It needs only a scale and your height, which is why it is used everywhere as a first screen. The <a href="../../calculators/body-fat.html">body-fat estimate</a> asks how much of your weight is fat, using a tape measure around the neck, waist, and (for women) hips. Each one fills a gap in the other.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>BMI</th><th>Navy body-fat estimate</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>What you need</td><td>Height and weight</td><td>Height and a tape measure</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What it measures</td><td>Weight relative to height</td><td>Estimated share of weight that is fat</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Main weakness</td><td>Can't tell muscle from fat</td><td>Sensitive to how you measure</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Best use</td><td>Quick screening</td><td>Tracking change over time</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>When the 2 disagree</h2>
    <p>A muscular person can have a BMI in the "overweight" range and a body-fat estimate in the athletic range. In that case BMI is likely misleading. The reverse also happens: someone with a "healthy" BMI can carry a higher share of fat, particularly around the waist, which tends to be more common with age as muscle declines. When the 2 tools point in different directions, the waist measurement is often the tiebreaker, because fat around the abdomen is the type most closely linked with health risk.</p>

    <h2>Tracking without obsessing</h2>
    <p>Both tools are most useful as occasional check-ins rather than daily numbers. Body weight swings by a few pounds day to day with water and food, and tape measurements vary with posture and breathing. Measuring once every few weeks, under the same conditions, shows the real trend.</p>
    <p>These are screening estimates for adults, not diagnoses. They don't apply in the same way to children, during pregnancy, or for some medical conditions. A clinician can put them in context with blood pressure, blood work, and family history, and anyone worried about their weight or eating should talk with a doctor or registered dietitian.</p>
  </div>
